 i saved a phrase  i saved a phrase and then i tagged it  i tagged it  you know according to the case that it  was either instrumental case  or you know accusative or genitive  maybe i got the odd one wrong but i  could go and look it up  so i got into the habit there of tagging  every single but every single noun every  single adjective but not as an  individual word but as a phrase  that way when i come across these  phrases again i see them plus i can go  to my vocabulary section and call up you  know locative case or instrumental case  and see all the examples  that i've saved and so i'm getting again  this concentrated  exposure to phrases where  one or and sometimes in some cases two  cases appear in the same phrase  i  it became it made me more aware  and i think that's what's so important  is to start to notice and to be aware  i should say first of all solomia's  corrections made me aware that i'm still  not as good as i'd like to be with my  case endings made me determined to do  something about it so now i'm i'm  tagging all of these and reviewing them  and what's more on the flight home i was  reading a book in ukrainian  that solomia had had given me a very  interesting book and there in pencil i  was marking up the book g for genitive l  for locative i for instrumental every  single  noun and adjective  i was noticing forcing myself to notice  what that case was  i have the feeling that those things if  i keep doing it are going to  help me  notice better be more aware  of these  endings and how they how they act in  phrases i think trying to save  individual words and tagging them is  less effective than saving the phrases  and of course that link in our  vocabulary review area you can review  just phrases uh you can also review you  know by whatever tag you have  i think this would be interesting to do  in other languages as well so in my  greek now i've gone back to greek  so i'm also trying to  tag the phrases for  genitive or accusative which in some  cases in greek is the same ending  and i think this can be done  for you know verbs in the romance  languages which cause so much trouble  uh if uh you can tag for you know first  purge you can tag it for person  and you can tag it  for tense  so that you can review for example just  the imperfect tense or just the simple  past  or a certain person and a certain tense  and review them in phrases now since i'm  not working on a romance language right  now  it's not something i'm going to do i'm  going to stick with ukrainian in greek  but if some of you want to try that i'd  be interested in hearing and again an  extension of this noticing and tagging  at link is to if you have a book that  you don't mind marking up to do the same  thing when you're reading in a book so  you're constantly forcing yourself to  notice these things  as you're reading now i must admit on  the plane i i did i would do this for a  while in my book that i was reading  and then i would not do it for a while  so that i'd kind of get more momentum  into reading the book and then i'd go  back to do it doing it and i i it's not  a matter of one or two days i think this  is something one has to keep on doing  for at least a couple of weeks but it  increases our our sensitivity our  awareness our ability to notice these  things which i expect especially we do  it with phrases will translate into an  ability to produce  these features of the language  more naturally so i just thought i'd  comment on that so again thanks to  solomia who by the way is a tremendous  teacher and i didn't want to be critical  of her and she certainly triggered  a consciousness  on my part  that i want to work harder to nail my  cases  and once i've done this with cases i may  go and do this the same with different  verbs of motion or perfective and  imperfect verbs those things that cause  me the most trouble in slavic languages  and i can do it in in czech and in  polish and in russian as well  so there you have it on noticing sort of  a sequel to the issue of of corrections  and again if you enjoy these videos  please subscribe  and i look forward to your comments and  i would be particularly interested if  some of you try to do this on link or at  least even marking up books and if you  feel that it  helps you  you know deal with those issues in  another language that are  often the most difficult because they're  different to what we have in our in in  the languages that we already speak  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

